# The Model Context Protocol at its tag 2026-07-28, `reference/mcp/2026-07-28/specification/`: each
# sentence that holds MUST, MUST NOT, or SHALL in the pages that a server is bound by, and what
# will hold it in `std.mcp`. A line is
#   <page>#<heading> | <first words of the sentence> | test <name>
# or
#   <page>#<heading> | <first words of the sentence> | reason <why std.mcp has nothing to do with it>
# where <page> is the path of the page there without `.mdx`, and <heading> is the title of the
# heading nearest above the sentence, or the title of the page before its first heading. The same
# words twice under one heading are told apart by `(2)` after the heading, from the second. A
# cell of a table that is a sentence is written as `<first cell of its row>: <the cell>`.
# `tests/mcp_standard.rs` fails for a sentence that is in no line, and for one that two lines name.
#
# Every line says `reason to be decided`: the work that builds `std.mcp` replaces it with a test,
# or with the reason the sentence binds nothing the module does, line by line.
#
# The pages taken are the 22 that `mcp_standard.rs` lists as TAKEN. The pages left out are the 8
# that it lists as LEFT_OUT, each with why.
#
# The words of requirement are those of the issue: MUST, MUST NOT, and SHALL. One sentence of the
# pages taken says REQUIRED instead, as RFC 2119 allows ("These headers are REQUIRED for
# compliance." under streamable-http#Standard Request Headers: Mcp-Method on every request, Mcp-Name
# on tools/call, resources/read, and prompts/get), and is no line here.
